}
\hr
}

\Babstract{
There should be a standard-library function
for decomposing floating-point numbers
into a sign bit, exponent, and integer significand.
}

\h2(listed=false){Contents}

\make_contents

\h2{Introduction}

The C++ standard library is currently lacking an elementary operation
for decomposing floating-point numbers into integers.
Such an operation is useful for a wide variety of applications,
internally present in standard libraries already,
and should be exposed to the user.

\h3{Refresher on floating-point model and representations}

To understand what it means to \q{decompose} a floating-point number,
one must first consider the mathematical model of floating-point numbers.
The C23 standard draft \ref("N3220")
describes the model of floating-point numbers in
§5.2.5.3.3 as follows:
\Bquote{
The following parameters are used to define the model for each floating type:

\style{
#float-parameters,
#float-parameters tr,
#float-parameters tbody,
#float-parameters td {
  border: none;
}

math[display=block] {
  font-size: 125%;
}
}
\table(id="float-parameters"){
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mi{s}}}
    \td{sign (\math{\mo{±}\mn{1}})}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mi{b}}}
    \td{
      base or radix of exponent representation
      (an integer \math{\mo{>}\mn{1}})
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mi{e}}}
    \td{
      exponent (an integer between
      a minimum \math{\msub{\mi{e}\mi{min}}} and
      a maximum \math{\msub{\mi{e}\mi{max}}})
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mi{p}}}
    \td{precision (the number of base-\math{\mi{b}} digits in the significand)}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\msub{\mi{f}\mi{k}}}}
    \td{nonnegative integers less than \math{\mi{b}} (the significand digits)}
  }
}

For each floating type, the parameters
\math{\mi{b}},
\math{\mi{p}},
\math{\msub{\mi{e}\mi{min}}}, and
\math{\msub{\mi{e}\mi{max}}}
are fixed constants.

For each floating type,
a \dfn{floating-point number} (\math{\mi{x}}) is defined by
the following model:

\mathblock{\mrow{
  \mi{x}
  \mspace(width="0.25em"){}\mi{=}\mspace(width="0.25em"){}
  \mi{s}
  \msup{\mrow{\mi{b}}\mrow{\mi{e}}}
  \munderover{
    \mi{∑}
    \mrow{\mi{k}\mi{=}\mn{1}}
    \mi{p}
  }
  \msub{\mi{f}\mi{k}}
  \msup{\mi{b}\mrow{\mi{−}\mi{k}}}
  \mi{,}\mspace(width="1em"){}
  \msub{\mi{e}\mi{min}}
  \mspace(width="0.15em"){}\mi{≤}\mspace(width="0.15em"){}
  \mi{e}
  \mspace(width="0.15em"){}\mi{≤}\mspace(width="0.15em"){}
  \msub{\mi{e}\mi{max}}
}}
}

Or grossly simplified,
one could say:
\mathblock{\mrow{
  \mi{x}
  \mspace(width="0.25em"){}\mi{=}\mspace(width="0.25em"){}
  \mi{s}
  \msup{\mrow{\mi{b}}\mrow{\mi{e}}}
  \mi{q}
  \mi{,}\mspace(width="1em"){}
  \mi{q}
  \mo{∈}
  \mi{ℕ}
}}

This model is not just a theoretical basis,
but closely matches how floating-point numbers are represented in memory;
the ISO/IEC 60559 standard defines several binary interchange formats
where a floating-point number is represented using
\ul{
  \li{a single sign bit (representing \math{\mi{s}})}
  \li{exponent bits (representing \math{\mi{e}}), and}
  \li{significand bits (representing \math{\mrow{\mi{q}\mo{=}\msub{\mi{f}\mi{k}}}}).}
}

Thus, many operations are implemented by decomposing floating-point numbers
into these components.

\Bex{
To implement \tcode{std::scalbn(double \math{\mi{x}}, int \math{\mi{n}})}
(which multiplies \math{\mi{x}} with
\math{\msup{\mi{b}\mi{n}}} given \math{\mi{n}}), one needs to
\ol{
  \li{decompose \math{\mi{x}} into its floating-point components}
  \li{adjust the exponent by \math{\mi{n}}, and}
  \li{recompose the floating-point number with the adjusted exponent.}
}
}

\Bex{
To implement \tcode{std::to_chars} with output in hex notation,
where \math{\mrow{\mi{b}\mo{=}\mn{2}}}, one needs to:
\ol{
  \li{decompose the input into its floating-point components,}
  \li{bit-shift and convert the significand (integer) to hexadecimal digits,}
  \li{add/remove trailing zeros and/or insert a decimal point, and}
  \li{print '\tt{p}' followed by the (adjusted) exponent in decimal.}
}

Of course, this is a gross simplification,
and decomposing the floating-point number happens in decimal notation too,
but there is a less direct relationship between the significand and the printed digits.
}

\Bex{
To implement \tcode{std::strong_order::operator()} for floating-point numbers,
one cannot simply use the builtin \tcode{<=>} operator
but needs to decompose floating-point numbers to ensure additional requirements such as
NaNs with negative sign bit comparing less than NaNs with positive sign bit.

While it is possible to implement such requirements
in terms of \tcode{std::signbit}, \tcode{std::isnan}, etc.,
some standard libraries decompose fully \q{by hand}
using \tcode{std::bit_cast} and various bitwise operations.
See
\ref("https://github.com/microsoft/STL/blob/da52dc0fcadbd83690cfd1ed32408d2847439493/stl/inc/compare#L423-L470"){\tt{microsoft/STL/.../stl/inc/compare}} and
\ref("https://github.com/gcc-mirror/gcc/blob/54e232d0d4752b419ed775bdad50d83798fa899d/libstdc%2B%2B-v3/libsupc%2B%2B/compare#L907-L961"){\tt{gcc-mirror/gcc/.../libsupc++/compare}}.
}

\h3{The problem}

Frustratingly, the decomposition described above is already implemented in standard libraries,
just not exposed to the user.
It is also difficult for the user to implement decompositions manually,
especially with good performance,
and especially in a way that portably supports exotic floating-point formats.

The standard library only offers much higher level operations such as
\tcode{std::frexp}
(which decomposes into a normalized fraction in ranges
\math{\mrow{\mo{±}\mo{[}\mn{0.5}\mo{,}\mn{1}\mo{)}}} and an exponent);
it does not provide the low-level building block that could be used to implement
\tcode{std::frexp}, \tcode{std::scalbn}, \tcode{std::to_chars}, etc.

We therefore propose adding a \tcode{std::decompose_float} function.

\h2{Motivation}

The key benefit of a \tcode{std::decompose_float} function
is that it allows the user to implement floating-point operations
without needing special knowledge about the layout of the floating-point type.

\Bex{
When implementing a conversion from a \tcode{float} to a \tcode{BigInt} type
(i.e. an arbitrary-precision integer),
one would need to split the \tcode{float}
into an integer \math{\mi{q}} and binary exponent \math{\mi{e}}, then
\cppblock{
return \tcode{BigInt(\math{\mi{q}}) * pow(\math{\mi{b}}, BigInt(\math{\mi{q}})}); // << \math{\mi{e}} for binary floats
}

This approach can be found in various arbitrary-precision arithmetic libraries in practice.
}

An additional benefit is that it acts as a \q{swiss-army knife}
for asking many questions about a floating-point number at the same time efficiently.

\Bex{
\ul{
  \li{Checking if a number is zero requires checking if the significand is zero.}
  \li{Checking if it's finite requires checking if the exponent has a certain value
  (for binary interchange formats).}
  \li{The NaN payload becomes extractable as the significand itself.}
  \li{Subnormals can be converted to integers without first needing to be normalized.}
  \li{\etc}
}

See \ref("#why-not-a-cmath-function") for a more detailed example.
}

Another way to look at \tcode{std::decompose_float} is that it performs a transition
from the floating-point domain into the integer domain,
which would be very difficult to implement correctly for the user by themselves.
This domain transition happens in the aforementioned \tcode{BigInt} construction,
and when printing floating-point numbers.

\Bex{
When printing floating-point numbers,
regardless of the type,
a transition into the integer domain makes it possible
to use the same underlying routine for all types with the same radix:
\cppblock{
void print_float( // universal runtime library function
  std::uint64_t significand,
  int exponent,
  bool sign,
  int precision
);

void print_float(float x, int precision) {
  auto dx = std::decompose_float(x);
  print_float(dx.significand(), dx.exponent(), dx.sign_bit(), precision);
}
void print_float(double x, int precision) {
  auto dx = std::decompose_float(x);
  print_float(dx.significand(), dx.exponent(), dx.sign_bit(), precision);
}
// support for various other types ...
}
Note that \tcode{std::uint64_t} is sufficient
for all formats in common use except \tcode{std::float128_t}.
}

\h2{Design}

The proposed API is as follows:

\cppblock{
template<class T> struct float_decomposition {
  // properties
  using value_type = T;
  using significand_type = \seebelow;

  // radix for the result of exponent()
  static constexpr int radix = numeric_limits<T>::radix;
  // lowest possible result of exponent()
  static constexpr int min_exponent = \exposid{implementation-defined};
  // greatest possible result of exponent()
  static constexpr int max_exponent = \exposid{implementation-defined};
  // signed integer width required to represent any exponent
  static constexpr int exponent_width = \exposid{implementation-defined};

  // greatest possible result of significand()
  static constexpr significand_type max_significand = \exposid{implementation-defined};
  // bit width of greatest possible significand() result,
  // which may be lower than the width of significand_type
  static constexpr int significand_width = bit_width(max_significand);

  // constructors
  constexpr float_decomposition() : float_decomposition(decompose_float(T{})) {}

  // accessors
  constexpr int              sign() const noexcept;        // returns -1 or 1
  constexpr int              exponent() const noexcept;    // returns base-radix exponent
  constexpr significand_type significand() const noexcept;
  constexpr explicit operator value_type() const noexcept;

  // boolean properties
  constexpr bool is_finite() const noexcept;               // std::isfinite(x)
  constexpr bool is_inf() const noexcept;                  // std::isinf(x)
  constexpr bool is_nan() const noexcept;                  // std::isnan(x)
  constexpr bool is_normal() const noexcept;               // std::isnormal(x)
  constexpr bool sign_bit() const noexcept;                // std::signbit(x)
  constexpr bool is_signaling() const noexcept;            // std::issignaling(x)
  constexpr bool is_subnormal() const noexcept;            // std::issubnormal(x)
  constexpr bool is_zero() const noexcept;                 // std::iszero(x)
};

template<class T>
constexpr float_decomposition<T> decompose_float(const T& x) noexcept; // freestanding
}

A lot of design questions can be quickly answered in a few sentences:

\dl{
  \dt{Why is \tcode{float_decomposition} not simply an aggregate?}
  \dd{
    Because this gives the implementation more flexibility regarding the layout,
    including the use of bit-fields.
    Also, structured bindings would be undesirable due to potential bugs
    from getting the order of the members wrong.
  }

  \dt{Why is it \tcode{constexpr}?}
  \dd{
    Most floating-point operations
    (but especially inspection operations like \tcode{std::isinf})
    are already \tcode{constexpr}, so this one should be as well.
  }

  \dt{Why is it freestanding?}
  \dd{
    The operation is elementary and does not raise any floating-point exceptions,
    nor does it interact with \tcode{errno}.
    There is no technical reason it couldn't or shouldn't be freestanding.
  }

  \dt{What is \tcode{significand_type}?}
  \dd{
    It is an implementation-defined unsigned integer type.
    This is needed because there are types such as \tcode{std::float128_t}
    where the result would not fit into other types like \tcode{unsigned long long}.
  }

  \dt{Why does \tcode{exponent()} return \tcode{int}?}
  \dd{
    Because \tcode{int} is sufficient for every real-world floating-point format,
    even if it has the minimum width of 16 bits.
    \tcode{std::float128_t} has only 15 exponent bits.
    The return type can be changed in the future should a floating-point format ever emerge
    that requires more than \tcode{int} can provide.
    \tcode{int} exponents are also widespread in the standard library already,
    such as in \tcode{\q{int numeric_limits<float>::max_exponent}}.
    Even if a format with more than 16 exponent bits emerged
    (such as an implementation of ISO/IEC 60559 256-bit binary interchange floating-point types),
    the platforms supporting such \q{ultra-precise floating-point types}
    are unlikely to have 16-bit \tcode{int}.
  }

  \dt{Why is \tcode{radix} provided as a static member?}
  \dd{
    Of all the \tcode{numeric_limits} properties,
    \tcode{radix} is the most important property in this context.
    It provides the minimum knowledge required for \tcode{float_decomposition}
    to be interpreted correctly.
    That justifies providing easy access to it.
  }

  \dt{What about all the other static constants?}
  \dd{
    These are helpful for metaprogramming since \tcode{int} and \tcode{significand_type}
    may be much larger than the actual required width.
    If the user has a guarantee that the significand is only, say,
    3 bits large (for FP8-E4M3),
    they might choose a different algorithm when subsequently processing
    \tcode{std::float_decomposition},
    even if \tcode{significand_type} is \tcode{unsigned int}.
    It is worth noting that these static constants provide information
    only about the integer results returned by
    \tcode{exponent()} and \tcode{significand()}
    rather than being an extension of \tcode{numeric_limits}.
  }

  \dt{What are \tcode{std::is_signaling}, \tcode{std::is_subnormal}, and \tcode{std::iszero}?}
  \dd{
    These are functions adopted from C23 via \ref("P3935R2").
    They are not available in C++26,
    but will almost certainly be available in C++29,
    likely before this paper is merged.
  }
}

More involved design questions are addressed below.

\h3{Handling of non-finite inputs}

\h4{Why non-finite inputs should be checked for}

Firstly, it should be noted that determining whether a number is finite
is typically extremely fast and would be a side product of the decomposition process.
ISO/IEC 60559 binary interchange floating-point types are
non-finite if and only if the exponent consists of all one-bits.

\Bnote{
The finiteness check for any binary interchange floating-point number boils down to
\cppblock{
if (raw_exponent == max_raw_exponent) [[unlikely]]
}
where \tcode{raw_exponent} needs to be extracted anyway as part of the decomposition,
and \tcode{max_raw_exponent} is a constant.
For \tcode{std::float64_t}, the added cost to the critical path is merely:
\codeblock("nasm"){
cmp     edx, 2047                  ; if (edx == 20247)
je      .skip_adding_integer_bit   ;   goto skip_adding_integer_bit;
}
See also \ref("#impl-decompose-float").
}

In the event that even this tiny check is too costly,
it can be eliminated via \tcode{[[assume(std::isfinite(x))]]} up front in theory,
although in practice, the assumption has no effect.
Another possible way to eliminate the check would be via a separate
\tcode{std::decompose_finite_float} function that has UB for non-finite inputs,
but this is currently not proposed.

Simply handling non-finite inputs as UB would be a safety hazard;
unlike in Rust, there is no way to disable certain checks in \q{unsafe mode},
so it's either always or never unsafe.
Infinities and NaNs can often arise unexpectedly at runtime,
so the potential for running into UB is great.
Treating NaNs as UB would also be unprecedented;
all of \header{cmath} provides functions with wide contracts
that handle non-finite inputs gracefully,
and this is the ISO/IEC 60559 approach in general.

\h4{Result of \tt{significand} and \tt{exponent} for non-finite inputs}

For ISO/IEC 60559 binary interchange types
the \q{obvious} non-finite behavior would be if
\ul{
  \li{
    \tcode{significand()}
    returned the contents of the significand representation
    reinterpreted as an integer.
    This is useful for examining NaN payloads.
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{exponent()}
    returned the exponent adjusted using the same bias as for finite numbers.
    This means that the exponent of \math{\mrow{\mo{±}\mi{∞}}}
    would be greater than that of any finite number,
    which makes comparisons of \tcode{exponent()} between results meaningful,
    unless NaN is involved.
  }
}
This behavior is \q{obvious} because it makes the original value
bitwise reconstructible from the decomposition, and
both infinities and NaN are handled identically using the same minimal logic
(see \ref("#impl-decompose-float")).

For ISO/IEC 60559 decimal interchange floating-point types,
infinity, qNaN, and sNaN are represented using the bit patterns
\tcode{11110}, \tcode{11111'0}, and \tcode{11111'1} respectively,
in the so-called \dfn{combination field},
but this combination field is \u{not} simply the exponent.
This makes it unclear
what the results of \tcode{exponent()} and \tcode{significand()} should be specifically,
and perhaps this issue would be better addressed when properly providing
support for decimal floating-point types in \header{cmath} and the rest of the standard library.
The paper leaves this part implementation-defined.

For any other floating-point types,
the results are implementation-defined
because we don't know their representation down to the last bit anyway,
so we cannot say what a reasonable mapping of values to \tcode{significand()}
and \tcode{exponent()} would be.
However, the implementation might still provide reasonable behavior
for types very similar to binary interchange types,
like x87 80-bit \tcode{long double}, \tcode{std::bfloat16_t}, etc.
and should be recommended to do so.

\h3{Why not a \tt{<cmath>} function?}

Another design option would be to go through WG14 and
standardize a quantized function
similar to \tcode{frexp}, like:

\cppblock{
double frexpq(double x, int* exp) {
  // sample implementation assuming that numeric_limits<double>::radix == 2
  double d = std::frexp(x, &exp);
  exp -= std::numeric_limits<double>::digits;
  return std::scalbn(d, std::numeric_limits<double>::digits);
}
}

\Bnote{
This is a naive implementation.
See \ref("#impl-frexpq") for a more plausible one.
}

Unlike \tcode{frexp},
which returns a normalized fraction,
this could return a number that is always a (small) integer.
The significand integer can then be obtained via \tcode{static_cast},
and that conversion would always be exact given an integer
with enough bits to represent the significand (except for non-finite inputs).

The key reason against this direction
is that \tcode{frexpq} does only a portion of the work.

\Bex{
Consider how to actually implement a \tcode{BigInt} constructor
as in the motivating example with \tcode{BigInt} in \ref("#motivation")
using the hypothetical \mark{\tcode{std::frexpq}}
vs. the hypothetical \mark{\tcode{std::decompose_float}}:

\cppblock{
struct BigInt {
  // typical BigInt implementations use a sign-and-magnitude representation
  bool sign;
  unsigned long* limbs;

  BigInt(std::unsigned_integral auto value, bool sign)
    : sign(sign), limbs(do_alloc_limb_array(value)) {}

#ifdef USE_FREXPQ
  BigInt(std::float64_t x) : BigInt(0, false) {
    if (!std::isfinite(x)) {
      throw std::invalid_argument("non-finite input");
    }
    int e;
    double xi = \mark{std::frexpq}(x, &e);
    *this = BigInt(static_cast<std::uint64_t>(std::fabs(xi)), std::signbit(xi));
    *this <<= e;
  }
#else
  BigInt(std::float64_t x) : BigInt(0, false) {
    auto d = \mark{std::decompose_float}(x);
    if (!d.is_finite()) {
      throw std::invalid_argument("non-finite input");
    }
    *this = BigInt(d.significand(), d.sign_bit());
    *this <<= d.exponent();
  }
#endif

  BigInt& operator<<=(int s) {
    if (s > 0) do_shift_left(s);
    if (s < 0) do_shift_right(-s);
    return *this;
  }
  // ...
};
}

The key observation is that \tcode{std::decompose_float} does the work
of four functions simultaneously:
\ul{
  \li{
    \tcode{std::frexpq}, by yielding the significand as an integer
    as well as the corresponding base-2 exponent;
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{std::isfinite}, by identifying infinities and NaNs by their exponent;
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{std::fabs}, by splitting the sign bit from the significand; and
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{std::signbit}, by extracting the sign bit separately.
  }
}
}

Miraculously, \tcode{std::decompose_float}
is also a potentially cheaper operation than \tcode{std::frexpq}
despite being vastly more useful.
Specifically, \tcode{std::frexpq}
would be required to normalize subnormal numbers
rather than merely adjusting the exponent like for normal numbers.
By comparison,
\tcode{std::decompose_float}
can reinterpret the significand of both zeros and subnormal numbers directly.

In summary, \tcode{std::decompose_float} is the better solution
for decomposing floating-point numbers because
\ul{
  \li{
    it provides a cleaner interface; and
  }
  \li{
    it is a swiss-army knife that tends to solve several problems simultaneously,
    like computing \tcode{std::frexpq}, \tcode{std::fabs}, and
    various boolean tests simultaneously; and
  }
  \li{
    it does not waste any work,
    whereas \tcode{std::frexpq} needs to test whether a number is subnormal or infinity
    but stores an unspecified value in \tcode{*e} for such inputs
    (perhaps it could be defined not to waste this work,
    but \tcode{std::frexp} stores an unspecified value,
    so \tcode{std::frexpq} should presumably behave the same); and
  }
  \li{
    it is easy to implement a variety of operations on top of \tcode{std::decompose_float},
    but not on top of \tcode{std::frexpq};
    for example, \tcode{std::frexpq}
    does not seem like it should be involved in \tcode{std::signbit},
    but \tcode{std::signbit(x)} is \tcode{std::decompose_float(x).sign_bit()}; and
  }
  \li{
    it has cheaper handling of subnormals
    because it can reinterpret the significand directly,
    suggesting that it is a lower-level or more elementary operation; and
  }
  \li{
    it is safer: since \tcode{std::frexpq} requires the user to perform conversion
    to an integer type themselves, it presents a safety hazard
    if a user chooses an insufficient type and the conversion results in UB.
  }
}

\h3{Exponent guarantee for binary interchange floating-point}

For binary interchange floating-point types
(\tcode{std::float32_t}, \tcode{std::float64_t}, possibly \tcode{float}, etc.),
the proposed utility becomes significantly more useful if there is a strict guarantee
that the result of \tcode{exponent()} is the biased exponent \math{\mi{E}}
with a constant offset,
so we should provide that guarantee.
Several interesting properties emerge:
\ul{
  \li{
    Subnormal numbers and zeros can be distinguished from normal numbers
    based on the exponent alone.
  }
  \li{
    Numbers with greater \tcode{exponent()} result are always greater in magnitude,
    which is helpful for establishing orderings such as \tcode{std::strong_order}.
  }
  \li{
    The underlying raw biased exponent \math{\mi{E}} can be recovered exactly.
  }
  \li{
    The exponent bias and exponent width of the format
    (which are not obtainable through \tcode{numeric_limits})
    become obtainable programmatically.
  }
  \li{
    There is only one possible choice for \tcode{significand()},
    so all results become fully independent of implementation choices.
  }
}

These properties are too interesting and useful to forfeit
by leaving the choice of \tcode{exponent()} up to the implementation.
The guarantee also doesn't impose any additional runtime cost;
see \ref("#impl-decompose-float").

\h3{Decimal floating-point}

C23 optionally standardized
\tcode{_Decimal32}, \tcode{_Decimal64}, and \tcode{_Decimal128},
which are ISO/IEC 60559 decimal interchange floating-point types.
It is plausible
that a C++ implementation would be interested
in providing these as an extension for C compatibility, or
that we would want to standardize extended floating-point types like \tcode{std::decimal64_t}.

A key difference to binary floating-point types is that decimal floats have several different
representations of the same mathematical value.
For example, \math{\mn{1.5}} can be represented as
\math{\mrow{\mn{15}\mo{×}\msup{\mn{10}\mn{-1}}}} but also as
\math{\mrow{\mn{150}\mo{×}\msup{\mn{10}\mn{-2}}}}.
That \math{\msup{\mn{10}\mn{-1}}} or \math{\msup{\mn{10}\mn{-2}}}
is called the \q{quantum}, and the exponent is called the \q{quantum exponent}.

\Bnote{
The choice of quantum exponent is not just arbitrary but can control whether
\math{\mn{1.5}} gets printed as \tcode{"1.5"} or \tcode{"1.50"},
like for \tcode{java.math.BigDecimal.toString()},
where the \tcode{"1.50"} format makes more sense if the number represents e.g. cents of a currency.
The C standard generally specifies not just the result
but also the quantum exponent of results of mathematical functions
because it is important information.
}

The design and wording of \tcode{std::decompose_float} acknowledges this problem by
requiring that when reconstructing the original floating-point value,
it has the same quantum exponent, and that \tcode{exponent()} returns the quantum exponent
instead of some arbitrary exponent.

\h3{Choice of header}

There should be a new header \header{decompose}
providing just \tcode{std::decompose_float} and \tcode{std::float_decomposition}
because no other header is a good fit:

\ul{
  \li{
    \header{cmath} contains a whole bunch of C mathematical functions,
    rather than low-level floating bit extraction.
    It is also not freestanding and has a connotation of C compatibility.
  }
  \li{
    \header{numeric} is way too heavy for this ultra-low-level freestanding utility.
    Fundamental floating-point utilities
    like \header{limits}, \header{stdfloat}, or \header{numbers}
    traditionally have their own headers.
  }
  \li{
    \header{limits} is focused on compile-time characteristics,
    not on runtime decomposition, and
    the name doesn't make sense for \tcode{std::decompose_float}.
  }
  \li{
    \header{stdfloat} provides only type aliases and is extremely lightweight,
    and it seems worth keeping it this way.
  }
}

\h3{Composing floating-point numbers}

The paper provides no corresponding operation for composing floating-point numbers,
although a \tcode{std::float_decomposition} object can be explicitly converted
back to the original floating-point value.
A possible signature for this could be:
\cppblock{
template<class F, std::unsigned_integral Uint>
  constexpr F compose_float(bool sign, int exponent, Uint significand);
}

There are a number of reasons for why this is not provided:
\ul{
  \li{
    There is less need for a composition operation
    because functions such as \tcode{std::scalbn}, \tcode{std::setpayload}, \tcode{std::nan}, etc.
    already provide the necessary functionality.
    By contrast, \tcode{std::frexp} is insufficient for fully decomposing floating-point numbers.
  }
  \li{
    Composition often takes separate paths for different categories anyway.
    For example, \tcode{std::from_chars} handles \tcode{"nan"}, \tcode{"inf"}, and \tcode{"0.0"}
    inputs separately,
    so a \q{monolithic} composition function is not needed for those use cases.
    By comparison, \tcode{std::decompose_float} is useful precise because it is monolithic;
    it decomposes the floating-point number,
    and boolean tests like \tcode{is_finite()} can be used to dispatch to various paths afterwards.
  }
  \li{
    ISO/IEC 60559 binary interchange formats encode NaNs
    as simply having the greatest possible exponent.
    This inevitably leads to two bad options:
    \ul{
      \li{
        Saying that certain \tcode{exponent} arguments make \tcode{std::compose_float} produce NaNs
        (i.e. making it a \q{monolithic} function)
        would make \tcode{std::compose_float} awfully \q{non-mathematical}.
        It would break the relation that the resulting magnitude is
        \tcode{std::scalbn(F(significand), exponent)}.
        Furthermore, if some large exponent value \math{\mi{N}} produces NaNs,
        what should \math{\mrow{\mi{N}\mo{+}\mn{1}}} produce?
        Supporting this form of NaN creation
        would result in a narrow contract that could otherwise be wide
        and could make the function a safety hazard.
        Furthermore, not every floating-point format represents NaNs in the same way,
        so saying that certain \tcode{exponent} arguments produce NaNs makes no sense for e.g.
        decimal floating-point types.
      }
      \li{
        If this form of NaN creation is not supported,
        \tcode{std::compose_float} is exactly equivalent to
        \tcode{std::copysign(std::scalbn(F(significand), exponent), sign ? -1 : 1)}.
        That makes the function syntax sugar at best and unmotivated at worst.
      }
    }
  }
}

Additionally, the \tcode{std::compose_float} function
suffers from a hard design problem regarding the parameter type of \tcode{significand}:
\ul{
  \li{
    If the significand type is templated as shown above,
    different argument types such as \tcode{unsigned int} and \tcode{unsigned long}
    result in different template instantiations,
    but this seems unnecessary given that we arguably need just one type, namely
    \tcode{float_decomposition<F>::significand_type}.
  }
  \li{
    Having a parameter of type
    \tcode{float_decomposition<F>::significand_type}
    makes the function hard to use with wider integers.
    For example, \tcode{std::bfloat_t} may have a significand type of \tcode{std::uint16_t}
    (it is a 16-bit type after all).
    Thus, while \tcode{std::bfloat_t} is able to represent 32-bit integer values like
    \math{\msup{\mn{2}\mn{20}}},
    \tcode{std::compose_float(false, 0, uint32_t{1} << 20)}
    would result in zero due to a lossy conversion \tcode{std::uint32_t → std::uint16_t}.
    The function would be virtually unusable in generic code where we may not know
    the integer types and floating-point types.
  }
  \li{
    Having a parameter of type \tcode{F} would solve the problem
    by moving the conversion to the call site and
    would be convenient due to deducing \tcode{F},
    but would make the function even less useful;
    it would simply be sugar for
    \tcode{std::copysign(std::scalbn(significand, exponent), sign ? -1 : 1)}.
  }
}

\h2{Wording}

The changes are relative to \ref("N5054").

