\h3(show_number=false){[float.decompose]}

Immediately following \eelis{numbers},
insert a neighboring subclause:

\Bins{
\cowel_html_element("h2"){Floating-point decomposition \stable_ref{float.decompose}}
}

\h3(show_number=false){[decompose.syn]}

Nested under \internal_ref{float.decompose},
insert a new subclause:

\Bins{
\cowel_html_element("h3"){Header \header{decompose} synopsis \stable_ref{float.decompose}}

\itemdecl{
//\serif{ all freestanding}
namespace std {
  //\serif{ \internal_ref{float.decompose.class}, class template \tcode{float_decomposition}}
  template<class T> struct float_decomposition;

  //\serif{ \internal_ref{float.decompose.function}, function template \tcode{decompose_float}}
  template<class T>
    constexpr float_decomposition<T> decompose_float(const T& x) noexcept;
}
}
}

\h3(show_number=false){[float.decompose.class]}

Nested under \internal_ref{float.decompose},
insert a new subclause.
The \mark{marked lines} are to be added only if
the corresponding \header{cmath} functions have been adopted from C23 via \ref("P3935R2").

\Bins{
\cowel_html_element("h3"){Class template \tcode{float_decomposition} \stable_ref{float.decompose.class}}

\itemdecl{
template<class T> struct float_decomposition {
  //\serif{ \internal_ref{float.decompose.properties}, properties}
  using value_type = T;
  using significand_type = \seebelow;

  static constexpr int radix = numeric_limits<T>::radix;
  static constexpr int min_exponent;
  static constexpr int max_exponent;
  static constexpr int exponent_width;

  static constexpr significand_type max_significand;
  static constexpr int significand_width = bit_width(max_significand);

  //\serif{ constructors}
  constexpr float_decomposition() : float_decomposition(decompose_float(T{})) {}

  //\serif{ \internal_ref{float.decompose.accessors}, accessors}
  constexpr int              sign() const noexcept;
  constexpr int              exponent() const noexcept;
  constexpr significand_type significand() const noexcept;
  constexpr explicit operator value_type() const noexcept;

  //\serif{ \internal_ref{float.decompose.boolean}, boolean properties}
  constexpr bool is_finite() const noexcept;
  constexpr bool is_inf() const noexcept;
  constexpr bool is_nan() const noexcept;
  constexpr bool is_normal() const noexcept;
  constexpr bool sign_bit() const noexcept;
  \mark{constexpr bool is_signaling() const noexcept;}
  \mark{constexpr bool is_subnormal() const noexcept;}
  \mark{constexpr bool is_zero() const noexcept;}
};
}

¶
\tcode{float_decomposition} objects
store information about individual components of
the value representation of an object of floating-point type \tcode{T}.
This value representation is called the \dfn{underlying value}
of the \tcode{float_decomposition} object.
Copying and assigning such objects copies and assigns their underlying value,
respectively.

¶
A specialization of \tcode{float_decomposition}
exists for every cv-unqualified floating-point type\iref{basic.fundamental}.
Each such specialization is a standard-layout, trivially copyable class type.

¶
A program is ill-formed
if it defines a specialization of \tcode{float_decomposition}
or instantiates a specialization \tcode{float_decomposition<T>}
where \tcode{T} is not a cv-unqualified floating-point type.

¶
Throughout this subclause,
requirements specific to
ISO/IEC 60559 binary interchange floating-point types,
also apply to \tcode{bfloat16_t}, if provided.
}

\h3(show_number=false){[float.decompose.properties]}

Nested under \internal_ref{float.decompose.class},
insert a new subclause:

\Bins{
\cowel_html_element("h4"){Properties \stable_ref{float.decompose.properties}}

\itemdecl{
static constexpr int min_exponent;
}

\itemdescr{
¶
The lowest possible result of \tcode{exponent()}.
}

\itemdecl{
static constexpr int max_exponent;
}

\itemdescr{
¶
The greatest possible result of \tcode{exponent()}.
}

\itemdecl{
static constexpr int exponent_width;
}

\itemdescr{
¶
The minimum required width for a signed integer type to represent
both \tcode{min_exponent} and \tcode{max_exponent}.
}

\itemdecl{
using significand_type = \seebelow;
}

\itemdescr{
¶
An implementation-defined unsigned integer type\iref{basic.fundamental}
capable of representing all possible results of \tcode{significand()}.
A specialization of \tcode{float_decomposition<T>} is ill-formed
if no such integer type exists.
}

\itemdecl{
static constexpr significand_type max_significand;
}

\itemdescr{
¶
The greatest possible result of \tcode{significand()}.

\note{
This includes results of \tcode{significand()}
where implicit bits have been concatenated to significand bits present
in the value representation of an underlying value.
If \tcode{T} is an ISO/IEC 60559 binary interchange floating-point type,
\tcode{significand_width} equals \tcode{numeric_limits<T>::digits + 1}.
}
}
}

\h3(show_number=false){[float.decompose.accessors]}

Nested under \internal_ref{float.decompose.class},
insert a new subclause:

\Bins{
\cowel_html_element("h4"){Accessors \stable_ref{float.decompose.accessors}}

\itemdecl{
constexpr int sign() const noexcept;
}

\itemdescr{
¶
\returns
\tcode{sign_bit() ? -1 : 1}.
}

\itemdecl{
constexpr int exponent() const noexcept;
constexpr significand_type significand() const noexcept;
}

\itemdescr{
¶
Let \math{\mi{x}} be the underlying value.
Let \math{\mi{b}} be \tcode{radix}.

¶
\effects
\ul{
  \li{
    If \math{\mi{x}} is finite,
    \tcode{exponent()} returns an integer \math{\mi{e}} and
    \tcode{significand()} returns an integer \math{\mi{m}}, such that
    \math{
    \mrow{
      \mo(form="prefix", fence=true, stretchy=false, rspace="0"){|}
      \mi{x}
      \mo(form="suffix", fence=true, stretchy=false, lspace="0"){|}
      \mo{=}
      \mi{m}
      \mo{×}
      \msup{\mi{b}\mi{e}}}
    }.
    Which pair of \math{\mi{e}} and \math{\mi{m}} is chosen
    is a property of the implementation,
    except that
    \ul{
      \li{
        \math{\mi{m}} is chosen to be exactly representable in type \tcode{T},
        \br\example{
        If \tcode{T} is \tcode{std::float16_t} and \math{\mi{x}} is \math{\mn{1}},
        the greatest possible result of \tcode{significand()}
        satisfying this requirement is \tcode{65'536},
        where \tcode{exponent()} returns \tcode{-16}.
        }
      }
      \li{
        if \tcode{T} is an ISO/IEC 60559 binary interchange floating-point type,
        \math{\mrow{\mi{e}\mo{=}\mi{E}\mo{−}\mi(mathvariant="italic"){bias}}}, where
        \math{\mi{E}} is the biased exponent encoded in \math{\mi{x}}, and
        \math{\mi(mathvariant="italic"){bias}} is the exponent bias constant for the format,
        as specified in ISO/IEC 60559.
        \br\note{
        Consequently, the following statements are true:
        \ul{
          \li{
            If and only if \math{\mi{x}} is zero or subnormal,
            \tcode{exponent()} returns \tcode{min_exponent}.
          }
          \li{
            There is only one possible choice of \tcode{significand()}.
          }
          \li{
            If for two \tcode{float_decomposition} objects \tcode{a} and \tcode{b},
            \tcode{a.exponent() > b.exponent()} is \tcode{true} and
            neither of their underlying values is NaN,
            \tcode{value_type(a) > value_type(b)} is \tcode{true}.
          }
        }
        }
      }
      \li{
        if \tcode{T} is an ISO/IEC 60559 decimal interchange floating-point type,
        \math{\mi{e}} is the quantum exponent of \math{\mi{x}}.
        \br\example{
        A floating-point value \math{\mn{1.5}}
        of an ISO/IEC 60559 decimal64 floating-point type
        can be decomposed
        so that \tcode{significand()} returns \tcode{15} and
        \tcode{exponent()} returns \tcode{-1}
        (corresponding to \math{\mrow{\mn{15}\mo{×}\msup{\mn{10}\mn{-1}}}}), or
        so that \tcode{significand()} returns \tcode{150}
        and \tcode{exponent()} returns \tcode{-2}
        (corresponding to \math{\mrow{\mn{150}\mo{×}\msup{\mn{10}\mn{-2}}}}),
        where \tcode{radix} equals \tcode{10}.
        The result of \tcode{exponent()} is equal to that
        of the C standard library function \tcode{llquantexpd64}.
        }
      }
    }
    \note{
    Ignoring \q{subnormal flushing},
    \math{\mi{x}} equals \tcode{copysign(scalbn(significand(), exponent()), T(sign()))}.
    }
  }
  \li{
    Otherwise, if \tcode{T} is an ISO/IEC 60559 binary interchange floating-point type,
    \tcode{exponent()} returns a result one greater than \tcode{exponent()}
    returns for any finite \math{\mi{x}}, and
    \tcode{significand()} returns the significand bits
    in the value representation of \math{\mi{x}},
    reinterpreted as an integer.
    \br\note{
    If \math{\mi{x}} is negative or positive infinity,
    \tcode{significand()} returns zero.
    If \math{\mi{x}} is NaN,
    \tcode{significand()} returns the NaN payload.
    }
  }
  \li{
    Otherwise, the result of \tcode{exponent()} and
    \tcode{significand()} is derived from \math{\mi{x}}
    in an implementation-defined manner.
  }
}

¶
\remarks
If two \tcode{float_decomposition} objects have the same underlying value,
their respective \tcode{exponent()} and \tcode{significand()} results are equal.

¶
\recommended
If \tcode{T} is not an ISO/IEC 60559 binary interchange floating-point type
but has similar representation,
implementations should return the same results
as if \tcode{T} were an ISO/IEC 60559 binary interchange floating-point type.

SEE ALSO: \ref("https://cstd.eisie.net/c23#7.12.15.4"){ISO/IEC 9899:2024 7.12.15.4}
}

\itemdecl{
constexpr explicit operator value_type() const noexcept;
}

\itemdescr{
¶
\returns
The underlying value.

¶
\remarks
Floating-point exceptions may be raised,
the conversion may be inexact,
and the NaN payload may not be preserved.
If \tcode{T} is an ISO/IEC 60559 decimal interchange floating-point type,
the result has the same quantum exponent as the underlying value.
\tcode{signbit(static_cast<value_type>(*this))} equals \tcode{sign_bit()}.
}
}

\editnote{
The \recommended paragraph addresses the case of x87 80-bit \tcode{long double},
which is not an ISO/IEC 60559 binary interchange floating-point type,
but is very similar to such a format;
it provides infinities and NaNs,
but has an explicit integer significand bit.
}

\h3(show_number=false){[float.decompose.boolean]}

Nested under \internal_ref{float.decompose.class},
insert a new subclause.
The \mark{marked lines} are to be added only if
